# Promotion log — {{CLIENT_NAME}}

Every write into the ontology repo. Append-only.

This log is how the requirement traceability map gets assembled, and it is what you show a client who asks why an entity exists. **Maintained at write time it costs nothing; reconstructed later it cannot be done accurately.**

| Date | What | Kind | Source ids | Repo path | Commit | Approved by |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | | entity / relationship / shape / read template / write template | REQ- / EV- | | | |

## Reversals

**Never silently remove a promoted object.** Something downstream cites it, and a dangling citation is how a build fails in a way nobody can trace.

| Date | What | Reason | Blast radius checked | Downstream decisions | Reported to |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | | | CQ / shapes / templates / eval cases / build tasks | | solution-architect |

## Version changes

| Date | Change | Compatibility class | Migration | Deprecation window | Client signed off |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | | additive / rename / grain-change / removal | | | |

A rename keeps the old term in the glossary as a synonym **permanently** — people will keep using it. A grain change is a new entity, not a change.
